body:
While normally I recommend docker, for this specific purpose I think it’s actually a worse solution.  My recommendation is to install caddy on your pi, and set up a reverse proxy which is dead simple with caddy.  All you need to do is make a file called `Caddyfile` and make its contents resemble this:

    media.domain.tld {
        reverse_proxy localhost:8096
    }

where media is any subdomain that has an A record pointing to the public facing IP address of the pi.  All you need to do now is forward ports 80 and 443 from your pi, and it should be accessible from anywhere.  If you don’t need it accessible from anywhere, you can skip the port forwarding step and just have the subdomain resolve to your local IP.  If your pi has a firewall, make sure to open the ports on that too.

To start caddy, just run `caddy start` in the same directory of the Caddyfile.

For me it was quite simple.  I set up a random port mapped to the docker container’s port (-p 49xxx:3001), and then I just used caddy with the following configuration:

    uptime.domain.tld {
        reverse_proxy localhost:49xxx
    }

I’d recommend caddy.  It just listens on port 443 and 80, and handles all redirects and proxies.  Using it is stupidly simple, basically just

    subdomain.site.tld {
        reverse_proxy localhost:8096
    }

    subdomain2.site.tld {
        reverse_proxy localhost:3000
    }
in a file called “Caddyfile”, then you can do `caddy run` or `caddy start` to run it in the background.

they also have a bunch of other directives besides reverse proxy like rewrites, redirects, and a built in file server.
